<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">State Shinto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kokka Shintō, 国家神道</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Imperial Japan</a>'s ideological use of the folk religion and traditions of Shinto, which emphasised the emperor as a divine being. It emerged at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji era</a> in 1868: the government called Shinto a non-religious patriotic practice and defined shrines as patriotic institutions serving state purposes such as honouring the war dead. The state controlled shrine finances and priests' training; with fewer shrines funded, nearly 80,000 closed or merged; by 1940 priests risked persecution for traditionally religious ceremonies. The term was introduced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the Shinto Directive of December 1945, which banned the state's ideological use of Shinto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-shinto.jpg" alt="State Shinto" width="330" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bank of the Empire of Japan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_government_small-face-value_paper_money_50_Sen_(Yasukuni_Shrine)_-_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
